\fs32\b NnPruneHsm: Reviewer Guide\b0\fs22\par
\par
\b 1. Overview\b0\par
NnPruneHsm is a research prototype for learning an initially over-parameterized heterogeneous neural network and reducing it to a compact graph under a validation-loss constraint. The implementation supports linear, sigmoid, centred-sigmoid and second-order (quadratic-input) neuron behaviour. The research workflow separates training, validation and final testing.\par
\par
The pruning transaction is:\par
1. Save a complete deep snapshot of topology and parameters.\par
2. Remove the least-magnitude candidate connections.\par
3. Delete hidden neurons with no active input or no active output.\par
4. Merge hidden neurons having exactly one active incoming and one active outgoing connection.\par
5. Validate the resulting graph.\par
6. Retrain the reduced graph for 10,000 stochastic sample updates using training data only.\par
7. Evaluate the fixed validation set without changing parameters.\par
8. Accept the reduction if its validation loss is within the configured limit; otherwise restore the complete snapshot.\par
\par
The 10,000 value denotes sample-update cycles, not 10,000 complete dataset epochs. The test split is not used to choose the topology.\par
\par
\b 2. Quick Start\b0\par
Recommended Windows project/data location:\par
\f1 X:\\NeuralnetHSM\\NnPruneHsm-Now\\Data\f0\par
\par
Build and run:\par
1. Open NnPruneHsm.sln in Visual Studio.\par
2. Select Debug or Release, x86, and build the solution.\par
3. Run NnPruneHsm.exe.\par
\par
Circle demonstration:\par
1. Click Sel Net. In the network window use Load Net and select:\par
\f1 X:\\NeuralnetHSM\\NnPruneHsm-Now\\Data\\HsmCircleNow.net\f0\par
If this saved topology is unsuitable, select HsmCircle.net or create a 2-input, 1-output network with hidden neurons.\par
2. Click Sel Data. Click Load Data and select:\par
\f1 X:\\NeuralnetHSM\\NnPruneHsm-Now\\Data\\HsmCircle.csv\f0\par
3. Click Train Net. Confirm that the training and validation losses decrease and that no NaN or infinity is reported.\par
4. Record the dense network's neuron count, connection count, validation loss and classification metrics.\par
\par
Pruning:\par
1. In the training form set the pruning factor. The default 1.1 permits a validation loss up to 1.1 times the original dense-network validation loss (10 percent relative increase).\par
2. Left-click Prune*. The program prunes candidate connections, cleans the topology, performs 10,000 training-sample updates, and then validates.\par
3. Wait for the pruning summary. Do not terminate the program during the 10,000-update regeneration stage.\par
4. Confirm that accepted attempts reduce connections and/or hidden neurons. Rejected attempts must restore the prior topology and parameters.\par
5. Right-click Prune* only when intentionally requesting restoration of the saved pre-pruning state.\par
6. Save the accepted network and retain the CSV logs from the executable's logs folder.\par
\par
\b 3. Controls and Interpretation\b0\par
Sel Net: create or load the neural-network topology.\par
Sel Data: generate or load observations.\par
Train Net: open the training, evaluation and pruning form.\par
Help: open this reviewer guide.\par
Train/Stop: start or stop ordinary dense-network training.\par
Prune*: initiate transactional structural pruning with post-pruning weight regeneration.\par
Pruning factor 1.1: accept final validation loss no greater than 110 percent of the fixed dense baseline.\par
\par
For classification, report genuine accuracy, precision, recall and F1 together with the confusion matrix. Do not describe 100 minus a regression error as classification accuracy. Also report connection sparsity, neuron sparsity, compression ratio and relative validation-loss change.\par
\par
\b 4. Reproducibility and Reviewer Checks\b0\par
Use a fixed random seed and the same train/validation/test split when comparing dense and pruned models. Pruning decisions must use validation data only. Report the final test result after all pruning and threshold decisions have been completed.\par
\par
A reviewer should verify:\par
- Evaluate and validation-loss functions do not change weights or biases.\par
- The analytical gradients pass finite-difference checks.\par
- Every accepted pruning attempt completes 10,000 sample-update cycles.\par
- A rejected attempt restores neuron count, connection count, endpoints, weights, biases and predictions.\par
- Input and output neurons are never removed.\par
- Hidden neurons with zero input or zero output are removed.\par
- A one-input/one-output hidden neuron is considered for contraction.\par
- Linear contraction uses the exact product weight and bias transformation.\par
- Nonlinear contraction is treated as an approximation and retained only after retraining satisfies the validation-loss constraint.\par
- Network topology validation passes before saving.\par
\par
\b 5. Mathematical Definitions\b0\par
For an ordinary connection into neuron j:\par
\f1 z_j = b_j + sum_i(w_ij y_i)\f0\par
For a quadratic-input neuron:\par
\f1 z_j = b_j + sum_i(w_ij (y_i - 0.5)^2)\f0\par
For a sigmoid output with binary cross-entropy:\par
\f1 delta_output = prediction - target\f0\par
\par
For exact contraction of a linear hidden neuron j between i and k:\par
\f1 w_ik(new) = w_jk w_ij\par
b_k(new) = b_k + w_jk b_j\f0\par
\par
Connection sparsity:\par
\f1 S_E = 1 - E_pruned / E_dense\f0\par
Neuron sparsity:\par
\f1 S_V = 1 - V_pruned / V_dense\f0\par
Compression ratio:\par
\f1 CR = P_dense / P_pruned\f0\par
\par
\b 6. Expected Evidence for Publication\b0\par
The circle dataset is a functional demonstration, not sufficient evidence by itself for a Q1 claim. Preserve logs and report repeated independent runs, mean and standard deviation, dense-versus-pruned predictive performance, topology size, compression, runtime and extracted equations. Compare against suitable MLP, polynomial/sparse regression, symbolic-regression and modern pruning baselines. Include ablations for neuron operators, pruning, and post-pruning retraining.\par
\par
The defensible contribution is a heterogeneous nonlinear network that is progressively simplified into a sparse, explicit mathematical model while controlling validation loss. Avoid claiming exact functional equivalence for contracted nonlinear neurons; their retention is empirical and validation constrained.\par
\par
\b 7. Files Produced for Review\b0\par
The logs folder contains training and pruning histories when logging is enabled. Exported neuron and connection CSV files describe the final graph, and the equation export provides a human-readable representation. Archive the input data, random seed, dense model, pruned model and logs together for every reported experiment.\par
\par
\b 8. Limitations\b0\par
This is a research prototype built on .NET Framework 4.0 and WinForms. Nonlinear one-input/one-output contraction is approximate. Results require repeated-seed evaluation and external baselines. The interface is intended for controlled experiments rather than unattended production deployment.\par
}
